      ub not in E.  We need a witness d < pu with db in E and dv not in E.
    Case B (ab edge): triple (a=u, b=v, c) with pos(c) > pv and uc in E.
      Before insertion ab was not in E, so no witness was required; after
      insertion ab is in E, so we need a witness d < pu with dv in E and
      dc not in E.
  The new edge as the witness edge db can only ADD witnesses, never remove
  them, so it cannot create a violation.

Local certification for deletion of (u,v), assume pu < pv:
  The deleted edge can break the property in two ways:
    Case 1 (ac edge): triple (a=u, b, c=v) with pu < pos(b) < pv and
      ub not in E.  The ac edge is gone, so the obligation is removed;
      this case can never create a violation.
    Case 2 (witness edge db): triple (a, b, c) with pu < pos(a) < pv,
      av not in E, ac in E, and the only witness d < a was u (i.e. uv in E
      and no other d' < a with d'v in E and d'c not in E).
  The deleted edge as the ab edge can only REMOVE obligations, never create
  violations.
  The symmetric cases with u and v swapped are handled by swapping.
